Subject: [PATCH 05938/11362] [outer-loop] run the TRM sequence-refiner pilot:
 gate fails, for a different reason than v1-v3

Pursued the second unbuilt alternative directly per operator request.
Built the more faithful version of the TRM hypothesis: refine a whole
K=8-action candidate window jointly toward a KNOWN winning target,
matching how TRM actually works on Sudoku (refine the whole candidate
jointly), rather than single-action behavioral-cloning classification.

Trained only on the 144 genuinely-won trajectories, correctly
cross-referenced against the raw corpus's own won field -- caught and
corrected an important distinction before building on the wrong signal:
level_progress=1.0 in the staged shards means "reached this session's
own highest recorded checkpoint," not "won the whole game," a weaker
proxy than the raw won field.

Caught a real data bug before it corrupted the result: cn04's action
IDs are recorded as word labels (ACTION1-ACTION6, RESET) rather than
the numeral strings most other games use -- every cn04 row was being
silently dropped by the action-vocabulary filter until this was found
and fixed (RESET->0, ACTIONn->n, this project's own action-space
convention). Verified the fix recovered all 144 won sessions correctly
before launching the real run.

Pre-registered gate (same discipline as the prior multi-seed test):
recursion supported only if p<0.05 on exact-full-window-match accuracy
in a majority of 3 held-out games (sk48/m0r0/cn04), 5 seeds each.

Result: gate fails again, 0 of 3 games significant. But the more
important finding is why: per-action accuracy for BOTH recursive and
non-recursive arms sits at or below rough chance level (~14% for a
~7-action vocabulary) across all three games -- a different failure
mode from the earlier pilots' class-imbalance-driven low headroom.
Predicting 8 steps ahead from a single static frame with zero
action-history context is very likely under-determined -- the same
frame plausibly precedes very different sequences depending on
invisible player intent.

Documented in docs/research-notes/trm-leave-one-game-out-pilot-results-
2026-07-05.md's "v4" section with the honest overall assessment: after
four attempts this week (v1-v3 properly ruled out with statistical
rigor, v4 inconclusive for a task-design reason), a reasonable point to
deprioritize further pilot iteration on this standalone-reimplementation
approach rather than keep varying the task framing. A genuinely
informative next test would need history/intent context, not another
framing variant.

